"Draft written to `/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-Aokigahara/f3a7dee1-ddd5-5966-b7a1-ff45901003d9/scratchpad/ENGINE_SELECTION_draft.md`. The body is about 3,080 words before the sources list, and `/home/user/Aokigahara` was not modified.\n\nThe recommended path is a Godot 4 native macOS app, rendering on Metal with MetalFX upscaling at a 30 fps cap. An engine-agnostic Python pipeline feeds it: from the Yamanashi 0.5 m LiDAR it builds the terrain, tree positions detected from the canopy height model (CHM), and baked canopy sky-visibility and sun-transmittance maps. Godot stays a thin runtime that can be swapped out. The runner-up is three.js r186 WebGPU in Chrome on the same pipeline. It wins if the owner needs a shareable link or Godot fails the warm Mac benchmark. All three judges chose the same winner and runner-up; their mean weighted scores were 7.4 and 6.5.\n\nA headless Godot 4.7.2 render of the repository's real data met 3 of the 5 revised lighting targets. The baked canopy map alone also met 3, so it carries the look and SDFGI only adds to it. No render reaches the warm-highlight target, and by eye the renders do not yet match the UE5.8 frames.\n\nThe realistic ceiling is about 75–90% of the lighting in frames g–j, within 150–200 m of the camera, at a warm 30 fps. The project needs about 5–15 GB of disk, mostly on an external SSD.\n\nThe previous `RESUME.md` claims the SDFGI render met 4 of 5 targets. On the revised metrics it meets 3; dynamic range is 8.18 stops against a floor of 8.5. The draft uses 3."
